  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Canadian Commercial obligated $879M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Canadian Commercial won 1,114 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $789K. Canadian Commercial obligations are down 37.1% year over year. Canadian Commercial showed a multiyear pattern of rising obligations from FY21 through FY25, moving from $626M in FY21 to $1.4B in FY25, before dropping to $879M in FY26. Canadian Commercial draws most of its FY2026 volume from a narrow set of NAICS markets. Canadian Commercial received $439M in Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing, $173M in Explosives Manufacturing, and $77.8M in Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing in FY2026 to date. Canadian Commercial also recorded smaller FY2026 obligations in Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing, Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing, and related transport and manufacturing categories. Canadian Commercial totals are partial because FY2026 is a fiscal year to date snapshot. The mix shows concentration in armored vehicles and explosives plus a secondary aerospace component business.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How large are Canadian Commercial’s current federal contracts?",
        "body": "Canadian Commercial obligated $879M in FY2026 to date. Canadian Commercial won 1,114 awards this fiscal year, with an average award size of $789K. Canadian Commercial obligations are down 37.1% year over year compared with the prior fiscal period."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where do Canadian Commercial’s obligations concentrate by market?",
        "body": "Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing is the largest market for Canadian Commercial at $439M in FY2026 to date. Explosives Manufacturing is the second-largest at $173M, followed by Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $77.8M, with additional FY2026 obligations spread across aircraft engines, navigation systems, chartered freight air transportation, pharmaceuticals, construction machinery, ammunition, and crane systems."
      }
